# Copy this file to infra/.env and fill it in. infra/.env is gitignored - it holds secrets.
#
#   macOS / Linux:       cp infra/.env.example infra/.env
#   Windows PowerShell:  Copy-Item infra\.env.example infra\.env
#
# This is experiment 2. It is a SEPARATE deployment from the pre-test, in its own resource group,
# with its own database and its own secrets. Do not reuse the pre-test's .env: the pre-test's
# database password belongs to a server this deployment will not create, and pointing this study at
# that server would put two different `behavioural` schemas in one database.

# --- Resource group (required) ---
# The group you created with `az group create`. It must already exist; the deploy does not create
# it, deliberately - a template that owns the group can delete it.
RESOURCE_GROUP=rg-exp2-feeling-heard

# --- Database password (required) ---
# YOU CHOOSE THIS. It is not looked up anywhere: this deployment creates its own Postgres server,
# and this is the admin password it is created with. On the first deploy it is set; after that the
# server keeps it, so save it somewhere you will still have it in March. It cannot be read back
# out of Azure.
#
# Rules: 8-128 characters, at least three of upper case, lower case, digit, special character.
# Must not contain the admin username. Avoid $ ` " and \ - PowerShell and bash both mangle them.
DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Optional - defaults to 'portaladmin'.
#DB_ADMIN_USER=

# --- Qualtrics handover secret (set this before a real run) ---
# A random string you invent. Qualtrics sends it as the X-Handover-Secret header when it registers
# a participant's condition. Without it, anyone who finds the study URL can mint a token and enter
# in whichever cell of the 2x2 they like, and those responses are indistinguishable from real ones
# in the data.
#
# Generate one:  macOS / Linux  ->  uuidgen
#                PowerShell     ->  [guid]::NewGuid().ToString()
#
# A different value from the pre-test's. They are different studies and a secret shared between
# them is a secret that has to be rotated in two Qualtrics surveys at once.
HANDOVER_SECRET=

# --- Researcher data view (set this before a real run) ---
# The password on <appUrl>/data, on /api/study/log behind it, and on /api/admin/** - which includes
# an endpoint that deletes every participant's data.
#
# The study itself has to stay open: participants arrive anonymously from Prolific and cannot log
# in. Every participant is redirected to this host, so the URL is not a secret - without a password
# here, any of them could open /data and read every other participant's Prolific ID, behavioural
# log and voice transcript, or POST the reset endpoint and wipe the study mid-run.
#
# Left empty, those paths are CLOSED rather than open, and the study still runs.
RESEARCHER_PASSWORD=

# Optional - defaults to 'researcher'.
#RESEARCHER_USER=

# --- Human agent console (required for a session to work) ---
# What the confederate signs in with to see who is waiting and what the AI assistant already heard.
#
# Separate from the researcher's credential on purpose, and the separation is the point rather than
# tidiness: the agent needs to read the queue, and does not need to be able to delete the study.
# If several people run sessions they share this one, which is fine - which agent handled which
# session is recorded from their own session log, not from who was logged in.
#
# Left empty, the console is CLOSED, which means the handover leg does not work at all. Set it.
AGENT_PASSWORD=

# Optional - defaults to 'agent'.
#AGENT_USER=

# --- OpenAI Realtime, for the AI leg of the call (required for the voice to work) ---
# Used server-side only, to mint the short-lived token the browser connects to the Realtime API
# with. The key itself never reaches the participant's browser.
#
# This is the MTEC Azure OpenAI deployment's key - ask Andreas or Joe for it. If you have to stand
# up your own, it must be an AZURE OpenAI resource, not api.openai.com: the ethics application
# rests on ETH's no-data-retention contract with Microsoft, and sending the call audio to OpenAI
# directly would be outside what was approved.
#
# Left empty, the container starts and the portal works, but the AI leg fails at the first token
# mint: the participant sees the assistant fail to connect. Test the voice before a session, not
# during one.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# The Azure OpenAI resource both halves of the voice leg talk to. Set this together with the key
# above whenever the key is not for the application's default resource
# (https://mtec-insurance-portal.openai.azure.com/openai/v1).
#
# Take it from the resource's "Keys and Endpoint" page and make it end in /openai/v1, e.g.
#   https://<your-resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1
#
# This one value covers both ends: the server mints the ephemeral token at
# <base>/realtime/client_secrets, and the browser is told to exchange SDP at <base>/realtime/calls.
# The browser's URL used to be written out separately in configuration/voice/client.json, where
# changing resources moved one and not the other - and a token minted by one resource presented to
# another fails as an authentication error about a key that is perfectly valid.
#
# It must be an AZURE OpenAI resource, not api.openai.com - see the note above.
#OPENAI_BASE_URL=

# --- The participant-to-agent call: nothing to set ---
# The voice call between the participant and the human agent runs on Azure Communication Services,
# which the deploy CREATES and reads the connection string from itself. There is no value for you to
# put here and no secret for you to carry - that is deliberate, since it is one fewer credential in
# circulation.
#
# The only thing to decide is where that service keeps its data, and the default is Switzerland, for
# the same reason the resource group is: the ethics application commits to ETH infrastructure. To
# change it, pass callDataLocation to the Bicep rather than setting anything here.
#
# Running the app on your own machine is the exception - there is no deploy to read it from, so set
# COMMUNICATION_CONNECTION_STRING in your shell from the Azure portal (the ACS resource -> Keys) if
# you want to test the handover locally.

# --- Easy Auth (leave BOTH empty for the real study run) ---
# Setting both puts an Entra login wall in front of the whole app. That blocks anonymous Prolific
# participants and breaks the Qualtrics iframe. Only useful while building, to keep the half-built
# study off the open internet - and note that the researcher and agent passwords above already
# protect everything that reads participant data, so this is rarely what you want.
AUTH_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
